The full comparison is the cost and performance of the finished working product. Cheaper individual dies do not automatically make a cheaper accelerator. Lesson 034 will develop this tradeoff.

5. Packaging capacity needs a denominator

Suppose two package designs use different interposer areas. A facility that processes a given area each month may produce fewer units of the larger design, even before accounting for yield or additional steps.

Here is an intentionally simplified example. Assume a line can process 100,000 area units per month, one product uses two area units per package, and final package yield is 90%. Ignore edge losses, product mix and other constraints.

That supports 50,000 package attempts and 45,000 good packages. If the next design uses 2.5 area units with the same yield, output falls to 36,000 good packages from the same area capacity.

This is a teaching model, not a conversion formula for reported CoWoS wafers per month. Actual flows require the relevant wafer basis, geometry, routing steps, tool mix and yields.

6. Why this connects to the rest of the stack

From upstream, packaging needs qualified logic dies, memory and materials. Downstream, a finished package still needs a board, power delivery, cooling and system testing.

Imagine more compute dies arriving while a package input remains scarce. The extra dies can wait in inventory. Imagine packaging expanding before a data center is ready. The finished accelerators can wait instead.

You should now recognize Lesson 031's separate manufacturing and deployment constraints. Packaging can be critical without being the binding constraint for every product or every month.

7. The analyst's questions

- Which package family and product does the capacity number describe?

- Is the figure installed capacity, qualified capacity, assembly attempts or good shipped units?

- What changes in product area, component count and yield as the next generation ramps?

- Are logic dies, memory, substrates and downstream deployment available at the same time?

The economic judgment comes after these questions. A technically necessary service may still face competition, customer bargaining and expensive capacity expansion. Scarcity today does not establish permanent pricing power.

Quick check — make a prediction

In the illustrative area model, capacity expands 25% to 125,000 area units, while package area grows from two to 2.5 and yield stays at 90%. How much does good-unit output grow versus the original product?

Answer: it stays at 45,000. The capacity increase is entirely absorbed by the larger package. The tempting mistake is to equate a 25% capacity headline with 25% more accelerators.

Remember these three things

- Packaging connects separately manufactured components into one usable product.

- The interposer and substrate serve different connection roles.

- Translate packaging capacity into good units for the specific product before comparing it with chip or memory supply.

